 ## Overview
-The NeuS weight function is a method for volume rendering that derives an unbiased, occlusion-aware weighting scheme from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) to reconstruct surfaces from multi-view images without explicit 3D supervision [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem that naive volume density derived directly from SDF values produces biased surface reconstructions, while simple normalization of SDF values fails to handle occlusions correctly [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its parent [VolSDF Volume Rendering](2551-volsdf-volume-rendering.md) by explicitly deriving an "opaque density" $\rho(t)$ that ensures the weight function peaks exactly at the zero-level set of the SDF, and from its sibling [U-density Function](3695-u-density-function.md) by using signed distances and a specific derivative-based opacity formulation rather than unsigned distances and monotonic decay [S1, unofficial][S2].
+The NeuS weight function is a volume rendering technique that derives an unbiased, occlusion-aware weight $w(t)$ from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) $f(x)$ to reconstruct surfaces from multi-view images without explicit 3D supervision [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem that naive volume density $\sigma(t) = \phi_s(f(p(t)))$ produces biased surface reconstructions, while simple normalization of S-density fails to handle occlusions correctly [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-- **Input**: A ray $p(t) = o + tv$ where $o$ is the camera center and $v$ is the unit direction vector; an SDF network $f(x)$; a color network $c(x, v)$; the number of samples $n$; and the inverse standard deviation parameter $s$ (used in sampling, not the final weight formula itself) [S1, unofficial].
